<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext is permitted, and that is deliberate rather than an oversight.

  This app is LAN-first: Music Assistant, Navidrome and Home Assistant almost never
  have TLS on a home network, and their addresses are not known at build time —
  servers are discovered over mDNS or typed in by hand. Android's <domain> element
  matches DNS labels by suffix and has no concept of CIDR, so "all of 10/8, 172.16/12
  and 192.168/16" cannot be expressed here. Restricting cleartext to private ranges is
  therefore not something this file can do, whatever it would be nice for it to say.
  If that restriction is wanted it has to live in the HTTP layer, where the address is
  known at the moment of the call.

  Trust anchors are SYSTEM ONLY, on purpose. Android has ignored user-installed CAs by
  default since API 24 because they are an MITM vector: anything that can get a
  certificate into the user store — malware, a configuration profile, a device
  management agent, a user tapped through a warning — can then read every byte this app
  sends. That includes the Music Assistant password, the Navidrome password and the
  Home Assistant long-lived token.

  Adding `<certificates src="user" />` would buy one thing: self-signed TLS on a LAN
  server. That case already has a plain answer here, which is plain HTTP over the LAN,
  permitted above. Trading an always-on credential-disclosure risk for a case that has
  a workaround is not a trade worth making — so it is not made, not even behind
  <debug-overrides>.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
